<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-power">
    <title>Power Management</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to reduce how much power an embedded system
            consumes when it has nothing to do.  Every program we have written this
            term runs the processor at full speed all the time, which is fine on USB
            power and wasteful on a battery.  The STM32C031C6 offers four low-power
            modes that stop parts of the chip when they are not needed, and one
            instruction, <c>__WFI()</c>, that parks the processor until an interrupt
            arrives.  The same chapter introduces the independent watchdog, a
            hardware timer that resets the processor if the program stops making
            progress.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter belongs to the Wednesday x-hour of the final week, and that
            hour is above all time to work on your final project.  The material here
            is taught as the schedule allows; it has no reading assignment and no
            homework, and nothing in the final project depends on it.  It is in the
            book because power management is part of nearly every real embedded
            design, and because the watchdog demonstration is worth five minutes of
            anyone's time.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Explain where a microcontroller's power goes: static leakage, and dynamic power proportional to the clock frequency.</li>
                <li>Name the STM32C031C6's four low-power modes and the trade-off that deepens from Sleep to Shutdown.</li>
                <li>Put the processor to sleep with <c>__WFI()</c> in an interrupt-driven main loop, and enter Stop mode with SLEEPDEEP.</li>
                <li>Explain what the independent watchdog does, and configure it with the key, prescaler and reload registers.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-power-day17x">
        <title>Day 17x In-Class: Project Work, and Power Management</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This x-hour is time to work on your final project.  Your paper design
                is due Friday at 10 pm, and the demos are on Thursday, so bring your
                system and keep building.  The material below, on sleep modes and
                the watchdog, is taught as time allows; when it is not taught in
                class, it remains here to read.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1: 6 min = 3 (where the power goes) + 3 (the levers, ST figure) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-power">
            <title>Part 1: Where Does the Power Go?</title>
            <p>
                An idle chip still consumes a little power, and every switching
                transistor consumes a little more.  The microcontroller's circuits
                are made of transistors acting as switches.  Even when a transistor
                is off, a tiny current leaks through it from the supply to ground;
                that is <term>static power consumption</term>.  To switch a
                transistor on or off, a tiny capacitance must be charged or
                discharged; that is <term>dynamic power consumption</term>, and it
                is proportional to the clock frequency, because every clock edge
                switches transistors.  For the Cortex-M0+ core, the dynamic power
                ranges from about 5 to 50 µW per MHz, depending on the fabrication
                technology.
            </p>
            <p>
                Three levers follow from those two facts.  Enable only the internal
                peripherals you are actually using, and disable them when done,
                because a peripheral whose clock is stopped consumes almost nothing;
                you have been enabling peripheral clocks through
                <c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR</c> and its siblings all term, and the same
                registers turn them off.  Run the processor at the lowest clock
                speed you can.  And let the processor sleep when it has nothing to
                do, using the low-power modes below.  The trade to understand from
                the start: the deeper the sleep, the longer it takes to restore the
                system to its operating state after waking
                (<xref ref="fig-power-st-modes"/>).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-st-modes">
                <caption>The STM32C0's operating modes, with typical current and
                wake-up time for each, at a supply of 3 V and 25 °C.  Run mode at
                48 MHz draws 58 µA per MHz.  Each mode below it draws less and
                takes longer to wake from: Sleep resumes in 10 clock cycles,
                Shutdown, at 19 nA, takes 385 µs.  Figure from
                STMicroelectronics.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide06_img1.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 10 min = 2 (clock tree) + 4 (the four modes, Table 19) + 2 (the worked Stop entry) + 2 (the loop) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-modes">
            <title>Part 2: The Four Low-Power Modes, and How You Enter One</title>
            <p>
                The STM32C031C6 contains many separate <term>clock domains</term>,
                each serving one part of the chip
                (<xref ref="fig-power-clock-tree"/>).  Stopping a clock disables the
                corresponding domain and saves its power.  The clock we have used
                all term is the HSI48, a 48 MHz internal oscillator whose output is
                divided by four at reset to give the 12 MHz system clock.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-clock-tree">
                <caption>Clock distribution in the STM32C0, from the Reference
                Manual.  The HSI48 oscillator at the left feeds the system clock
                through a divider, set to divide by four at reset; other
                oscillators, including the 32 kHz LSI that clocks the watchdog,
                serve their own domains.  Each gate in the tree is a place power
                can be saved.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide07_img1.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The low-power modes stop progressively more of the chip.  The
                device transitions from Run mode into a low-power mode and back;
                there is no path from one low-power mode to another
                (<xref ref="fig-power-transit"/>).  In <term>Sleep</term> mode the
                CPU clock is off and everything else can keep running: any
                peripheral can wake the CPU when an interrupt occurs.  In
                <term>Stop</term> mode the high-speed oscillators stop too; SRAM
                and register contents are retained, and waking re-enables the
                HSI48.  In <term>Standby</term> mode the core's supply domain is
                powered off and SRAM and register contents are lost, while the LSI
                and the watchdog can keep running.  In <term>Shutdown</term> mode
                everything is off, the current is nanoamps, and waking is
                equivalent to a reset.  The Reference Manual's Table 19,
                <em>Device resources enabled in different operating modes</em>,
                lists exactly what runs and what can wake the device in each mode
                (<xref ref="fig-power-resources"/>).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-transit">
                <caption>The low-power mode transit diagram, Figure 7 of the
                Reference Manual.  Every low-power mode is entered from Run mode
                and exits back to Run mode.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide08_img1.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-resources">
                <caption>Reference Manual Table 19: which resources run in each
                operating mode, and which can wake the device.  Reading down the
                columns, Sleep keeps everything but the CPU available; Stop keeps
                SRAM, the LSI and a few wakeup-capable peripherals; Standby and
                Shutdown keep almost nothing.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide09_img1.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Entering a mode takes at most three steps, and the selection is
                determined by the SLEEPDEEP bit and the LPMS bit field, as the
                Reference Manual's Table 20, <em>Low-power mode entry overview</em>,
                lays out (<xref ref="fig-power-entry"/>).  Plain Sleep mode needs no
                register writes at all: with SLEEPDEEP clear, executing
                <c>__WFI()</c> (wait for interrupt) stops the CPU clock until an
                interrupt arrives.  Stop mode is one bit more.  The LPMS field in
                <c>PWR_CR1</c> keeps its reset value of 000, which selects Stop;
                the SLEEPDEEP bit in the Cortex-M0+ system control register is set;
                and <c>__WFI()</c> enters the mode.  The PWR peripheral's
                registers, like every peripheral's, are reachable only while its
                clock is enabled, so the sequence is:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    RCC->APBENR1 |= RCC_APBENR1_PWREN;    // clock the PWR peripheral
    // PWR->CR1's LPMS field stays at its reset value 000, which selects Stop mode
    SCB->SCR |= SCB_SCR_SLEEPDEEP_Msk;    // deep sleep rather than Sleep
    __WFI();                              // enter Stop mode; an interrupt wakes us
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Waking is the same event in reverse: an appropriate interrupt
                occurs, the HSI48 restarts, execution resumes at the instruction
                after <c>__WFI()</c>, and the program clears SLEEPDEEP if the next
                sleep should be a plain one.  The deeper modes, Standby and
                Shutdown, are selected by writing 011 or 1XX into LPMS; their
                entry rows are in <xref ref="sec-power-reference"/>, and neither is
                needed this term.  There is a second entry instruction,
                <c>__WFE()</c> (wait for event), which wakes on events as well as
                interrupts; <c>__WFI()</c> is the one we use.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-power-entry">
                <caption>Reference Manual Table 20: the low-power mode entered for
                each combination of SLEEPDEEP, LPMS and entry instruction.  Sleep
                needs only SLEEPDEEP clear; Stop is SLEEPDEEP set with LPMS at
                000; Standby and Shutdown use LPMS 011 and 1XX.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide11_img1.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The place <c>__WFI()</c> belongs in a program is the main loop of
                an interrupt-driven design, which you have been writing since the
                timer chapter.  Initialize the devices, enable the interrupts, and
                then loop: check the flags your ISRs set, take whatever action they
                call for, and go to sleep until the next interrupt.  At least one
                enabled interrupt must exist, or nothing will ever wake the
                processor.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
int main(void) {
    // Initialize devices, enable interrupts
    ...
    while (1) {
        // Check interrupt flags, take action
        ...
        // Go to sleep, await the next interrupt
        __WFI();
        // When the CPU wakes up, execution resumes here and the loop repeats
    }
    return 1;
}
// ISRs (including at least one interrupt that awakens the processor!)
...
]]></code></program>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 17 min = 3 (what it does) + 9 (the quick example, both conditions) + 2 (the reveal) + 3 (the configuration) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-watchdog">
            <title>Part 3: The Watchdog</title>
            <p>
                A deployed embedded system has nobody watching it.  If the program
                gets stuck, in a loop that never exits or waiting for an event that
                never occurs, the system simply stops responding, and on a shelf or
                a rooftop there is no one to press the reset button.  The
                <term>independent watchdog</term> (IWDG) is the hardware answer.
                It is a 12-bit downcounter with an 8-bit prescaler, running on its
                own 32 kHz internal clock (the LSI) that is independent of the main
                clock, which is what the name means: the watchdog keeps counting
                even if the rest of the chip's clocks fail.  A healthy program
                reloads the counter regularly, which the tradition calls
                <term>petting the dog</term>.  If the program gets stuck and the
                reload stops coming, the counter reaches zero and the watchdog
                resets the processor.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-watchdog-dog">
                <caption>The watchdog at rest.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide16_img1.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17x-watchdog">
                <title>A quick example</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>The program below is <c>watchdog.c</c>.  Make a copy of your
                    template project, call the copy <c>Watchdog</c>, and copy
                    <c>watchdog.c</c> from Canvas into the new project's source
                    folder.  Compile, upload to your Nucleo, and open CoolTerm.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-observe">
                    <statement><p>You'll see a <c>Hello dog world</c> message in
                    CoolTerm that seems to repeat every 4 seconds.  Predict what the
                    message's reappearance means before going on.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-press-often">
                    <statement><p>Press the blue user button repeatedly, more
                    frequently than every 4 seconds, and observe what
                    happens.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-press-rarely">
                    <statement><p>Now press it less frequently than every 4 seconds,
                    and observe the difference.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                What actually happens: the <c>Hello dog world</c> message is
                printed at the very start of the program, before the event loop, so
                seeing the message over and over means the device is rebooting
                regularly.  When you press the blue user button, the program is
                petting the watchdog, thereby resetting the countdown that would
                otherwise have ended in a reboot.  If you pet the watchdog
                frequently enough, the program continues running without
                rebooting.
            </p>
            <p>
                The configuration steps are visible in the listing, and each one is
                a write to one of the watchdog's registers (Reference Manual
                §20).  Writing 0x0000 5555 to the key register <c>IWDG_KR</c>
                unlocks the protected prescaler and reload registers.  The
                prescaler <c>IWDG_PR</c> sets the counting rate; 0b011 divides the
                32 kHz LSI by 32, giving a 1 ms tick.  The reload register
                <c>IWDG_RLR</c> sets where the countdown starts; 4000 ticks is
                4 seconds.  Writing 0x0000 CCCC starts the watchdog, and writing
                0x0000 AAAA reloads the counter, which is the pet.  Once running,
                the IWDG cannot be stopped.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#include <stdio.h>
// A few magic numbers for the watchdog (from the datasheet)
#define KEY_ACCESS 0x00005555
#define KEY_UPDATE 0x0000AAAA
#define KEY_START  0x0000CCCC
int main( void ) {
    uart2_init();
    printf( "Hello dog world\r\n");
    // Use the built-in blue user button as a proxy for petting the dog.
    RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOCEN;
    GPIOC->MODER = (GPIOC->MODER & ~GPIO_MODER_MODE13_Msk) | (GPIO_INPUT << GPIO_MODER_MODE13_Pos);
    // The watchdog is always accessible, so no need to turn any clocks on for it.
    // First, unlock the watchdog registers by writing a special value to the key register
    IWDG->KR = KEY_ACCESS;
    // Set up the prescaler - the watchdog clock runs at 32 kHz.  We will set up a 1 ms tick.
    while( IWDG->SR & IWDG_SR_PVU ); // Can only update when the PVU status bit is clear.
    IWDG->PR = 0b011;
    // Reload register is set to 4000: we need to pet the dog at least once every 4 secs
    while( IWDG->SR & IWDG_SR_RVU ); // Can only update when the RVU status bit is clear
    IWDG->RLR = 4000;
    // Wake the dog...
    IWDG->KR = KEY_START;
    // Now, loop and pet the dog whenever the blue user button is pushed.
    while(1){
        if ( (GPIOC->IDR & GPIO_IDR_ID13) == 0 ){
            // Pet the dog
            IWDG->KR = KEY_UPDATE;
            printf( "Nice Doggy\r\n");
            delay_ms(100);  // be nice to the terminal.
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Two refinements exist beyond what we use.  The IWDG has a window
                option, which generates a reset if the dog is petted too early as
                well as too late.  And the STM32C031C6 carries a second watchdog,
                the system window watchdog (WWDG), which runs on the main clock and
                can generate an early interrupt before resetting; it is described
                in Reference Manual §21.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4: 2 min stretch briefing, then ~15 min of project work -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day17x-stretch">
            <title>Part 4: A Super Quick Modification, then Project Time</title>
            <activity xml:id="act-day17x-needier">
                <title>Super quick modification</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-onesec">
                    <statement><p>Make the dog even needier: have it need a pet
                    every 1 second.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day17x-limit">
                    <statement><p>Make it needier still.  Add a <c>printf()</c>
                    statement after the watchdog initialization but before the
                    <c>while</c> loop, so it runs before you get a chance to pet
                    the dog.  How needy can you make it before it resets too fast
                    to even print your message?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day17x-needier">
                <p>The 1 second dog is <c>IWDG-&gt;RLR = 1000;</c> with the same
                prescaler.  For the limit: at 9600 baud a character takes about
                1.04 ms (ten bit times per frame), so a 17-character message such
                as <c>Hello dog world\r\n</c> takes about 18 ms to leave the
                USART.  A reload value much below about 20 resets the chip in the
                middle of the message, and the terminal shows a partial line
                repeating.  Students will find the edge is not sharp, because the
                reset and restart add time of their own.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                The rest of the hour is time to work on your project.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-power-reference">
        <title>Reference: The Low-Power Modes and the Watchdog</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Lookup material for the four modes and the IWDG.  Nothing here is
                new; it is the same information arranged for a quick look-up.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-power-ref-modes">
            <title>The Four Modes, and Their Entry Values</title>
            <p>
                The modes and what survives in each (Reference Manual §4.3, and its
                Table 19): <term>Sleep</term> stops the CPU clock only, and any
                interrupt wakes it in a few cycles.  <term>Stop</term> also stops
                the HSI48 and HSE oscillators; SRAM and registers are retained, the
                LSI and LSE can keep running, and exiting re-enables the HSI48
                with the system clock at its divide-by-four reset setting.
                <term>Standby</term> powers off the core's supply domain; SRAM and
                register contents are lost, except the PWR control and backup
                registers, while the IWDG and LSI can keep running.
                <term>Shutdown</term> disables all oscillators and the supply
                monitor; contents are lost, and exiting behaves like a reset.
            </p>
            <p>
                Entry is controlled by SLEEPDEEP (in the Cortex-M0+ system control
                register, <c>SCB-&gt;SCR</c>) and LPMS[2:0] (in <c>PWR_CR1</c>,
                <xref ref="fig-pwr-cr1"/>), then executing <c>__WFI()</c> or
                <c>__WFE()</c> (Reference Manual §4.3.2, Table 20):
            </p>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Mode</cell>
                    <cell>SLEEPDEEP</cell>
                    <cell>LPMS[2:0]</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>Sleep</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>any</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Stop</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>000 (the reset value)</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Standby</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>011</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Shutdown</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>1XX</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                The PWR peripheral's clock is enabled with
                <c>RCC-&gt;APBENR1 |= RCC_APBENR1_PWREN;</c> before <c>PWR_CR1</c>
                is written, following the same rule as every peripheral this term.
                Waking from Sleep or Stop resumes execution after the
                <c>__WFI()</c>; the pending flag of the interrupt that caused the
                wake must be cleared as usual.  Waking from Standby or Shutdown
                restarts the program from the beginning, as after a reset.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-pwr-cr1">
                <caption>The PWR_CR1 register, from the Reference Manual.  The
                LPMS[2:0] field in bits 2 to 0 selects the low-power mode entered
                when SLEEPDEEP is set: 000 Stop, 011 Standby, 1XX Shutdown.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day17x-Sleep_Modes/slide12_img1.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-power-ref-iwdg">
            <title>The IWDG Recipe</title>
            <p>
                The independent watchdog is configured through four registers
                (Reference Manual §20.4).  All key values are written to
                <c>IWDG_KR</c>: 0x0000 5555 unlocks the prescaler, reload and
                window registers; 0x0000 CCCC starts the watchdog, whose counter
                counts down from 0xFFF; 0x0000 AAAA reloads the counter from
                <c>IWDG_RLR</c> and prevents the reset.  The prescaler
                <c>IWDG_PR</c> divides the 32 kHz LSI: 000 is divide by 4, each
                step doubles it, 011 is divide by 32 (a 1 ms tick), and 110 and 111
                are divide by 256.  <c>IWDG_PR</c> may only be changed while the
                PVU bit of <c>IWDG_SR</c> is clear, and <c>IWDG_RLR</c> while RVU
                is clear, which is what the two <c>while</c> loops in
                <c>watchdog.c</c> wait for.  The timeout is the reload value times
                the prescaled tick.  Once running, the IWDG cannot be stopped; it
                continues through Sleep and Stop modes, so a program that sleeps
                longer than its watchdog timeout will be reset in its sleep.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>
</chapter>
